          <SocialProof />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#naturally-gluten-free" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Is Protein Powder Gluten-Free?</a>
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#label-vs-certified" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Label vs Certified</a>
          <a href="#celiac-math" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. How Much Gluten Is Too Much</a>
          <a href="#ingredients" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Ingredients to Watch</a>
          <a href="#cross-contact" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Cross-Contact</a>
          <a href="#by-type" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Risk by Protein Type</a>
          <a href="#check-a-tub" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Check Any Tub</a>
          <a href="#sensitivity-allergy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Sensitivity, Allergy, Testing</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Whey, casein, egg, pea and rice protein are all naturally gluten-free, so most protein powders on the shelf contain no gluten ingredients at all. That is not quite the same as being safe for celiac disease. The risks are cross-contact in shared facilities, a handful of flavors that add cookie pieces, oats or malt, and the gap between a brand writing "gluten-free" on its tub and an independent certifier testing to a stricter limit. The best gluten-free protein powder for someone with celiac disease is therefore a certified one with a short label. Here are six options, ranked by how much verification stands behind them, and how to check any tub yourself.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="naturally-gluten-free" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Is Protein Powder Gluten-Free? Usually, With Caveats
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gluten is a group of proteins found in wheat, barley and rye, and in foods made from them. None of the common protein sources in powders come from those grains. Whey and casein come from milk, egg protein from egg whites, and pea, rice, soy, pumpkin and hemp proteins from plants that contain no gluten. A plain whey isolate or pea isolate is gluten-free by nature, which is why so many tubs carry the claim.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three things complicate the picture. The first is added ingredients. A minority of flavors include real cookie or brownie pieces made with wheat flour, oat-based ingredients, or barley malt, any of which can bring gluten into an otherwise clean powder. The second is cross-contact: a powder made on the same line as a wheat-containing product, or from a crop grown in rotation with wheat, can pick up traces of gluten even though no gluten ingredient is listed. The third is verification. A brand can print "gluten-free" on a label based on its own judgment, while a third-party certifier tests the product against a stated limit and audits the facility.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people avoiding gluten for comfort or preference, a powder labeled gluten-free with no wheat, barley, rye or oat ingredients is plenty. For people with celiac disease, where small but regular gluten exposure can damage the small intestine even without obvious symptoms, verification matters more. That is the distinction this guide is built around.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are avoiding gluten because of digestive symptoms and have not been tested for celiac disease, read the last section before you go further, because the order in which you do things matters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked: Verification Over Claims
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not run gluten tests for this guide. The picks come from label analysis, published certifications and the research on gluten thresholds in celiac disease, using the method described on our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test page</a>. Where we mention taste or mixing, we are reporting what buyers commonly say.
          </p>

          <p>
            We ranked products first by the strength of the gluten-free evidence. Third-party gluten-free certification, from programs such as GFCO or NSF, came first, because those programs test products and inspect facilities rather than relying on the brand's word. Next came products labeled gluten-free with short ingredient lists and no high-risk ingredients. Within each tier, we weighed protein per serving, general third-party testing such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice, and how many other ingredients a celiac buyer would need to vet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every pick had to be free of wheat, barley, rye, malt and oat ingredients in the flavors we looked at, and fully disclose its formula with no proprietary blends. We excluded powders whose gluten-free status we could not find stated anywhere, even if they are probably fine, because guessing is exactly what celiac buyers need to avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            One honest limitation: certifications are product- and sometimes flavor-specific, and retailer listings do not always agree on which certifier applies. For each pick we tell you what we found and where it came from. Before you buy, check the seal on the tub itself, and if it matters for your health, confirm in the certifier's online directory.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Gluten-Free Protein Powders, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nutrition figures and certifications below are per the label and brand at the time of writing and can vary by flavor, size and production run. Gluten-free certification in particular can apply to some flavors and not others, so check the seal on the exact tub you buy. The first two picks carry third-party gluten-free certification; the rest are labeled gluten-free by the brand and suit most people avoiding gluten, with notes on where celiac buyers should look harder.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
