        <div id="label-vs-certified" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Gluten-Free Label vs Certified Gluten-Free
          </h2>

          <p>
            In the United States, the FDA allows a food to be labeled "gluten-free" if it contains less than 20 parts per million of gluten and is either inherently free of gluten or made from ingredients processed to remove it. The rule covers FDA-regulated foods, including dietary supplements. Using the label is voluntary, and the FDA does not test products before they go on sale. The manufacturer is responsible for making sure the claim is true.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third-party certification adds independent checks. The Gluten-Free Certification Organization, known as GFCO, requires certified products to test at 10 ppm or less, half the FDA limit, and reviews ingredients and manufacturing practices. NSF runs its own gluten-free certification, and some other programs set limits as low as 5 ppm. A certified product carries the certifier's mark, and you can usually confirm it in the certifier's public database.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a person with celiac disease, the difference is less about the number and more about the process. A product that meets 20 ppm is well within what the research considers safe for most people, as the next section shows. What certification adds is confidence that someone outside the company checked, and that the facility has controls to keep the result consistent from batch to batch.
          </p>

          <p>
            A third category is worth noting: products that make no claim at all. A plain unflavored whey or pea isolate with no gluten claim may well be gluten-free, but without a statement from the brand you have no way to know how it was handled. Our <a href="/what-is-in-protein-powder/">guide to what is in protein powder</a> covers how to read the rest of the label.
          </p>

