        <div id="ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Ingredients to Watch: Oats, Malt, Cookie Pieces and More
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most protein powder labels are easy to clear, but a few ingredients deserve a second look. In the US, wheat must be declared as an allergen, but barley, rye and oats are not on the major allergen list, so a missing "Contains: wheat" statement does not rule out gluten.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oats and oat-based ingredients</h3>

          <p>
            Oats do not contain gluten themselves, but they are very often contaminated with wheat or barley during growing and processing, and a small share of people with celiac disease react to oat protein too. Some plant powders add oat powder or oat milk for texture. Orgain's Protein + Oatmilk line, for example, lists organic hydrolyzed oat powder, and the listings we found did not say whether the oats are tested gluten-free. If a powder contains oats, look for the words "gluten-free oats" or a certification seal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Barley malt and malt extract</h3>

          <p>
            Malt, malt extract, malt syrup and malt flavoring are usually made from barley and contain gluten. They show up occasionally in chocolate and cereal-style flavors and in some weight gainers. Maltodextrin is a different ingredient: despite the name, in the US it is usually made from corn and is gluten-free.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cookie, brownie and cereal pieces</h3>

          <p>
            Flavors built around real baked pieces are the most common way gluten ends up in a whey powder. Some markets' labels for Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard Cookies & Cream, for example, list wheat flour in the cookie pieces. If a flavor name sounds like a dessert, read its ingredient list even if other flavors from the same brand are gluten-free.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wheat protein and grass powders</h3>

          <p>
            Hydrolyzed wheat protein and wheat protein isolate appear in a few blends and are not safe for celiac disease. Wheatgrass and barley grass in greens-style blends can be gluten-free in theory but are a contamination risk.
          </p>

