        <div id="cross-contact" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cross-Contact: Facilities, Shared Lines and "May Contain"
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cross-contact is the main reason a product with no gluten ingredients can still test positive for gluten. Supplement makers often produce many products in one facility, and powders are especially prone to drifting dust and residue on shared equipment. A whey isolate made on the same line as a cookies-and-cream flavor, without thorough cleaning and testing in between, can pick up traces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Statements such as "made in a facility that also processes wheat" or "may contain wheat" are voluntary in the US and not standardized. Some companies use them liberally to cover any shared building, while others say nothing even when lines are shared. That makes them a weak signal in both directions. One retailer listing for Dymatize ISO100, for example, notes a facility that also processes wheat, yet the product is listed as gluten-free, which may mean the company tests to the FDA standard despite the shared building.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant proteins add another source of cross-contact: the field. Peas, lentils and other crops are often grown in rotation with wheat or barley and handled with the same equipment. A frequently cited 2010 US study of naturally gluten-free grains and flours found that several samples exceeded 20 ppm, with soy flour among the worst. Protein isolation removes much of the starch along with stray grain, but it is why certified plant powders are worth the premium for celiac buyers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best defense is a product whose maker tests finished batches. Certification is the easiest way to know that is happening. Failing that, a brand that publishes or will share its gluten test results is a better bet than one that simply prints the claim. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">guide to protein powder safety</a> covers how to ask brands for testing data.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Whey, Plant, Egg or Collagen: Gluten Risk by Protein Type
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whey isolate is generally the lowest-risk starting point. Milk is not grown alongside grain, dairy facilities are often separate from cereal processing, and isolates tend to have short labels. The main things to check are dessert flavors and any added fillers. If you also have a sensitive stomach, which is common in people newly diagnosed with celiac disease, an isolate is lower in lactose; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">protein powder for sensitive stomach guide</a> covers that overlap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant proteins carry slightly more cross-contact risk from the field and from shared grain-handling equipment, which is why certification counts for more here. Single-source pea or rice protein is easier to vet than a blend with many components. Avoid plant blends that include oat protein or ancient grains unless the tub is certified. For a wider look at the category, see our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder rankings</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Egg white protein is naturally gluten-free and usually a single ingredient, which makes it simple to vet. Collagen peptides are also gluten-free by nature, but collagen is an incomplete protein, with no tryptophan and little leucine, so it should not be your main protein source; our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen protein powder guide</a> explains its limits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mass gainers and meal replacement shakes deserve the most scrutiny. They often add carbohydrate sources, including oat flour, malt or other grain-based ingredients, and long ingredient lists give more room for a gluten source to slip in. If you use one, choose a certified product or read every line.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="check-a-tub" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Check Any Tub in Five Minutes
          </h2>

          <p>
            You will not always find one of our picks on the shelf, so it helps to have a routine for judging any protein powder. It takes about five minutes and works for any brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the front and back of the tub. Look for a gluten-free certification mark from GFCO, NSF or another program, and note whether the claim applies to this flavor. If there is only a plain "gluten-free" statement, that is still useful: the brand is asserting the product meets the FDA limit. If there is no gluten statement at all, treat the product as unknown.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, read the full ingredient list for wheat, barley, rye, malt, oats, wheat protein and baked pieces. Check the allergen statement and any facility warnings. Then look up the product in the certifier's online directory if it has a seal, because certifications lapse and formulas change. Reformulations are common, and a tub bought last year may not match this year's.
          </p>

          <p>
            If anything is unclear, email the brand and ask three questions: does it test finished batches for gluten, to what limit, and is the product made on lines shared with gluten-containing products. A clear, specific answer is a good sign; a vague one is a reason to choose a certified product instead. If you bake with protein powder, run the same check on every flour and mix you add, since a certified powder in a wheat-based recipe gains you nothing; our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-baking/">protein powder for baking guide</a> has more on that.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Certified first, labeled second, no claim never.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">For celiac disease, choose a third-party certified powder when you can, a gluten-free-labeled powder with a short ingredient list when you cannot, and skip products that make no gluten statement at all.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="sensitivity-allergy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Gluten Sensitivity, Wheat Allergy and Getting Tested
          </h2>

          <p>
            Celiac disease, non-celiac gluten sensitivity and wheat allergy are different conditions, and they call for different levels of care. Celiac disease is an autoimmune condition in which gluten triggers damage to the small intestine, and strict lifelong avoidance is the treatment. Wheat allergy is an immune reaction to wheat proteins that can cause hives, swelling or, rarely, anaphylaxis, and it requires avoiding wheat specifically. Non-celiac gluten sensitivity describes people who feel better without gluten but test negative for celiac disease and wheat allergy, and many of them tolerate trace amounts.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you think gluten is causing your symptoms, get tested for celiac disease before cutting it out. The standard blood tests and biopsy depend on you still eating gluten, and going gluten-free first can produce a false negative, leaving you without a diagnosis. Talk to a doctor about testing, and about whether family members should be screened, since celiac disease runs in families.
          </p>

          <p>
            After a diagnosis, a registered dietitian who works with celiac disease is worth seeing. Protein powder can be a convenient part of a gluten-free diet, especially for people who struggle to eat enough protein while relearning what is safe, but it is a supplement, not a fix. Ongoing symptoms on a strict gluten-free diet need medical follow-up, not a different powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide is not medical advice. Anyone with celiac disease, a diagnosed allergy, kidney disease, a pregnancy or medication that affects digestion should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding regular protein shakes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a Brand Because One Flavor Is Gluten-Free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gluten-free status often varies by flavor within the same line. Vanilla and chocolate may be clean while cookies and cream or a cereal flavor uses wheat-based pieces. Check the ingredient list and seal for the exact flavor and size you buy, every time you switch, rather than assuming the whole brand is safe.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming No Wheat Warning Means No Gluten</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                US allergen labeling requires wheat to be declared, but barley, rye and oats are not major allergens. A powder with barley malt or regular oats can carry no "Contains: wheat" statement at all. Read the full ingredient list for malt, oats and barley, not just the allergen box at the bottom.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlooking Oat Ingredients</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oats are a popular way to add texture and a "natural" feel to plant powders, but standard oats are frequently contaminated with wheat and barley. Unless the label says gluten-free oats or the product carries a certification seal, treat oat powder, oat milk powder and oat protein as a gluten risk if you have celiac disease.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Going Gluten-Free Before Testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cutting out gluten before a celiac test can make the blood test and biopsy come back falsely negative. If you suspect gluten is the problem, see a doctor and get tested while still eating gluten. A confirmed diagnosis changes how strict you need to be and whether family members should be screened.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Everything Else in the Shake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A certified gluten-free protein powder does not help if you blend it with regular oats, malted milk powder or a granola topping. Check every ingredient in a smoothie or protein bake, including flours, cereals and mix-ins. The protein is often the least risky part of the recipe.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best gluten-free protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For celiac disease, a third-party certified powder is the safest choice. Garden of Life Sport Organic Plant-Based Protein is listed as NSF certified gluten-free, and Ascent 100% Whey carries a certified gluten-free claim with the GFCO mark on several listings. For general gluten avoidance, labeled gluten-free isolates such as Isopure Zero Carb or Dymatize ISO100 are good options.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey protein gluten-free?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Whey itself is gluten-free because it comes from milk. Most plain whey isolates and concentrates contain no gluten ingredients. The risks come from added flavor ingredients such as cookie pieces or malt, and from cross-contact in shared facilities. Choose a whey that is labeled or certified gluten-free and check the specific flavor's ingredient list.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does certified gluten-free mean on protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It means an independent organization has tested the product and reviewed how it is made. GFCO, one of the best-known programs, requires products to test at 10 ppm of gluten or less, half the FDA labeling limit. Certified products carry the certifier's mark, and you can usually confirm the certification in the program's online directory.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a gluten-free label safe for celiac disease?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people with celiac disease, yes. The FDA gluten-free label means less than 20 ppm of gluten, which works out to well under a milligram per scoop, and research suggests most people with celiac disease tolerate under 10 mg a day. Certification adds independent verification, which is worth it for products you use daily.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which protein powder ingredients contain gluten?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Watch for wheat flour in cookie or brownie pieces, wheat protein, barley malt, malt extract, malt flavoring and regular oats. Maltodextrin is usually corn-based in the US and gluten-free. Wheatgrass and barley grass are a contamination risk. Because barley, rye and oats are not major allergens, always read the full ingredient list, not just the allergen statement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are plant protein powders gluten-free?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pea, rice, soy, pumpkin and hemp proteins are naturally gluten-free, but plant crops can pick up gluten from fields and equipment shared with wheat and barley. That makes certification more valuable for plant powders. Avoid plant blends with oat protein or grain ingredients unless the product is certified gluten-free.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use protein powder for baking on a gluten-free diet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, as long as the powder is gluten-free and every other ingredient in the recipe is too. Unflavored whey isolate, egg white and pea protein work well in gluten-free baking. Pair them with certified gluten-free flours and oats, and check baking powder, chocolate chips and mix-ins, which occasionally contain gluten.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
